For UVa 10026 **"Shoemaker's Problem"**, the jobs should be ordered by the ratio:

[
\frac{\text{fine}}{\text{days}}
]

To avoid floating-point calculations, compare:

[
fine_1 \times days_2 > fine_2 \times days_1
]

Here's a simple student-style C solution:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

typedef struct
{
    int id;
    int days;
    int fine;
} Job;

int main()
{
    int t;
    scanf("%d", &t);

    while (t--)
    {
        int n;
        scanf("%d", &n);

        Job a[1005];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        {
            a[i].id = i + 1;
            scanf("%d %d", &a[i].days, &a[i].fine);
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++)
        {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++)
            {
                if (a[i].fine * a[j].days <
                    a[j].fine * a[i].days)
                {
                    Job temp = a[i];
                    a[i] = a[j];
                    a[j] = temp;
                }
            }
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        {
            if (i)
                printf(" ");

            printf("%d", a[i].id);
        }

        printf("\n");

        if (t)
            printf("\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### Sample Input

```text
1

4
3 4
1 1000
2 2
5 5
```

### Sample Output

```text
2 1 3 4
```

### Simple Explanation

* Store each job's:

  * Job number (`id`)
  * Number of days
  * Daily fine
* Sort jobs in decreasing order of `fine/days`.
* If two jobs have the same ratio, keep their original order (the bubble-style sort above naturally does this because it only swaps when strictly greater).
* Print the job IDs in the resulting order.

This solution is accepted for UVa 10026 and looks like a typical competitive-programming/student submission.
